# Cursor Rules for MealScanner Mobile App

## TypeScript & Code Quality
- Always use explicit TypeScript types, avoid `any`
- Use strict TypeScript settings and enable all strict checks
- Prefer interface over type for object shapes
- Use const assertions for immutable data: `as const`
- Always destructure props in functional components
- Use proper return types for functions, especially async functions

## React Native & Expo Specific
- Use StyleSheet.create for styles instead of inline style objects
- Prefer functional components with hooks over class components
- Follow expo-router file-based routing conventions
- Import React Native components explicitly: `import { View, Text } from 'react-native'`
- Use expo-router's `useRouter()` and `useLocalSearchParams()` for navigation
- Always consider safe area insets using `useSafeAreaInsets()`
- Use proper Expo conventions for app.json configuration

## Import Organization
Group imports in this order:
1. React and React Native core
2. Expo libraries  
3. Third-party libraries
4. Local components and utilities
5. Types and interfaces
6. Relative imports

Example:
```typescript
import React from 'react';
import { View, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';
import { useRouter } from 'expo-router';
import { SomeThirdPartyLib } from 'third-party';
import { ThemedView } from '@/components/ThemedView';
import { Colors } from '@/constants/Colors';
import type { UserType } from './types';
```

## Component Structure
- Keep components focused and under 200 lines
- Extract complex logic into custom hooks
- Use TypeScript interfaces for all component props
- Include proper error boundaries for screen components
- Use meaningful component and file names that describe their purpose
- Prefer named exports over default exports for utilities and hooks

## Styling & UI Guidelines
- Use TailwindCSS classes primarily for styling
- Fall back to StyleSheet.create for complex styles or animations
- Use icons instead of emojis throughout the UI
- Maintain consistent green theme across all components
- Follow mobile-first responsive design principles
- Ensure touch targets are minimum 44px for accessibility

## Mobile UX Best Practices
- Always test and handle keyboard avoidance for input screens
- Use proper accessibility labels with `accessibilityLabel` and `accessibilityHint`
- Consider loading states and error states for all async operations
- Implement proper pull-to-refresh patterns where appropriate
- Use haptic feedback appropriately with HapticTab component

## State Management & Data Flow
- Use React hooks (useState, useEffect, useContext) for local state
- Keep state as close to where it's used as possible
- Use custom hooks to share stateful logic between components
- Prefer derived state over storing computed values
- Handle async operations with proper loading and error states

## Navigation & Routing
- Follow the (tabs) folder structure for tab-based navigation
- Use proper TypeScript types for route parameters
- Implement proper deep linking support
- Handle navigation edge cases (back button, tab switching)

## Performance Guidelines
- Use React.memo() for expensive components that re-render frequently
- Implement proper list virtualization for long lists (FlatList/VirtualizedList)
- Optimize images with proper resizing and caching
- Use lazy loading for non-critical components
- Profile and measure performance improvements

## Error Handling
- Implement error boundaries for each major screen/section
- Provide meaningful error messages to users
- Log errors appropriately for debugging
- Handle network errors gracefully with retry mechanisms
- Validate user inputs with proper feedback

## Code Organization
- Group related files in feature-based folders when appropriate
- Use index files to create clean import paths
- Keep utility functions in separate files with clear naming
- Use constants for magic numbers and strings
- Implement proper environment configuration

## Testing Guidelines
- Write unit tests for utility functions and custom hooks
- Test component rendering and user interactions
- Mock external dependencies and API calls
- Test error scenarios and edge cases
- Use proper test naming conventions

## Security & Privacy
- Never commit sensitive data or API keys
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Implement proper input validation and sanitization
- Follow secure coding practices for data handling
- Consider user privacy in data collection and storage

## Git & Version Control
- Use meaningful commit messages with conventional commits format
- Keep commits focused and atomic
- Use proper branch naming conventions
- Review and test changes before committing
- Use .gitignore to exclude generated and sensitive files 